ected":false,"custom":{"model":"914/4","year":1970,"category":"Misc Models","description":"The Porsche 914 was first shown at the 1969 Frankfurt Auto Show was, as intended, a true conglomeration. The front suspension was largely derived from the 911 with some VW components, and the interior was a blend of both companies’ parts bins. The initial engine offering was Volkswagen’s 80-hp fuel-injected 1.7 liter flat four, while the 914/6 had a twin-carburetor 2.0-liter Porsche flat six tuned for 125 hp.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970-Volkswagen-Porsche-914-4-0000818-14.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970-Volkswagen-Porsche-914-4-0000818-14.jpeg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1970-porsche-914/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X914.6..2.0L.","x":1970,"y":3.045,"name":"914/6 (2.0L)","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"914/6 (2.0L)","year":1970,"category":"Misc Models","description":"The standard 914 was powered by Volkswagen’s horizontal four-cylinder engine, producing a power output of 80 hp. Even with the lightweight Porsche body, acceleration suffered. The solution to this was to offer a second version: the 914/6, powered by a six-cylinder engine, total power output exceeded 100 hp.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Porsche-9146-1.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Porsche-9146-1.jpeg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1970-porsche-9146/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X917.LH.70","x":1970,"y":5.09,"name":"917 LH-70","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"917 LH-70","year":1970,"category":"Race Cars","description":"With the aerodynamic instability of the 917 in the 1969, two separate configurations were used in 1970. These were the short-tail Kurzheck version and the less common Langheck or long-tail. Most of the 917’s accolades were achieved by the 917 Kurzheck, leaving the Langheck a less popular, but ultimately just as potent contender.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970_Porsche_917Langheck-1-1024.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970_Porsche_917Langheck-1-1024.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1970-porsche-917-lh/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"Tapiro.Concept","x":1970,"y":0,"name":"Tapiro Concept","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"Tapiro Concept","year":1970,"category":"Concept Cars","description":"Giugiaro's 4th prototype at Italdesign was this radical Tapiro.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970_Porsche_TapiroConcept1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1970_Porsche_TapiroConcept1.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-tapiro-concept/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X917.K.71","x":1971,"y":4.865,"name":"917 K-71","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"917 K-71","year":1971,"category":"Race Cars","description":"For the 1971 Season, the 917 Kurzheck Coupé (917K) was upgraded in several ways","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/M13_3204.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Porsche-