s.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1972_Porsche_91710-0-1024-1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1972_Porsche_91710-0-1024-1.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1972-porsche-917-10/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X911.Carrera.RS.2.7.Lightweight","x":1973,"y":3.91,"name":"911 Carrera RS 2.7 Lightweight","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 Carrera RS 2.7 Lightweight","year":1973,"category":"911 Models","description":"Of all the 1580 Carrera RS 2.7s, only 200 were made were ordered with this lightweight ‘Sports’ trim which made the car more responsive and purposeful. In many ways these few cars were the ultimate road-going Porsche of the 1970s. Known as the Sports, Lightweight or even the M471 option code, these cars had improved the power-to-weight ratio. Reports of 75kg were stripped from the standard model by fitting lightweight body panels and lightweight glass. As a result, the Lightweight was a nimble 900kg car compared to its Touring counterpart at 975 kg.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/42-1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Porsche-911-Carrera-RS-2.7-Lightweight-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-carrera-rs-2-7-lightweight-1973/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"2,700 cc","Power":"213 cv","Torque":"255 Nm","Top speed":"241 km/h","0–100 km/h":"5.7 s","Production":"200"}}},{"id":"X911.Carrera.RS.2.7.Touring","x":1973,"y":4,"name":"911 Carrera RS 2.7 Touring","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 Carrera RS 2.7 Touring","year":1973,"category":"911 Models","description":"Revealed at the 1972 Paris Auto Show, the Carrera 2.7 RS was a special model used to homologate the 911 in Group 4 racing. Developed from the 911S, the 2.7 was more potent in almost every area. Compared to the standard Carrera, the 2.7 RS featured a larger engine, wider flares to accommodate the Fuchs alloy wheels, stiffened suspension, larger brakes and a ducktail rear spoiler.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/ffffff.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/1973-Porsche-911-Carrera-RS-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-carrera-rs-2-7-touring-1973/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"2,700 cc","Power":"213 cv","Torque":"255 Nm","Top speed":"245 km/h","0–100 km/h":"5.8 s","Production":"1,380"}}},{"id":"X911.Carrera.RSR.2.8","x":1973,"y":4.09,"name":"911 Carrera RSR 2.8","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 Carrera RSR 2.8","year":1973,"category":"911 Models","description":"The first 911 to ever wear the RSR badge and homologated for racing by the 1973 911 Carrera RS","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/c5e82eb739f89970d9a434be2947c4c9-1000.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Porsche-911-Carrera-RSR-2.8-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-carrera-rsr-2-8-1973/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"2,800 cc","Power":"284 cv","Torque":"292 Nm","Top speed":"286 km/h","0–100 km/h":"5.8 s","Production":"55"}}},{"id":"X911.Turbo.Prototype","x":1973,"y":0,"name":"911 Turbo Prototype","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 Turbo Prototype","year":1973,"category":"Concept Cars","description":"In the seventies Porsche was serious about turbocharging and this prototype signaled the way for later cars like the 930 and the RSR 3.0.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1973_Porsche_911TurboPrototype1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1973_Porsche_911TurboPrototype1.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1973-porsche-911-turbo-prototype/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X914.4.2.0","x":1973,"y":3,"name":"914/4 2.0","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"914/4 2.0","year":1973,"category":"Misc Models","description":"The initial offering gave those buyers the choice of a fuel-injected 1.7-liter Volkswagen four, which was anemic and universally derided, or the carbureted, overhead-cam, 2.0-liter horizontally opposed six. The latter engine was peaky and could be made to do great things–sixth overall at the 24 Hours of LeMans in 1970, for example–but its low take rate demanded a more suitable replacement. This came in 1973, when the fuel-injected variant of Volkswagen’s air-cooled Type 4 engine was dropped in behind the two seats, staying there through 1976, when series production ended. (The engine continued on in the 912E, which succeeded the 914 as Porsche’s entry-level car.) The short-stroke, overhead-valve powerplant displaced 2.0 liters (1971 cc) and made its 100 hp at 5000 rpm, whereas the six had made 110 hp at 5800 rpm. Yet the four matched the six’s torque output of 118 lb-ft, achieving this figure at 3500 rpm instead of 4200 rpm. And it was lighter in weight.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1973-914-4-2.0-2.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1973-914-4-2.0-2.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1973-porsche-914-4-2-0/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X917.20.Turbo","x":1973,"y":5