ture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X935.2.0..Baby.","x":1977,"y":4.045,"name":"935/2.0 ‘Baby’","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"935/2.0 ‘Baby’","year":1977,"category":"911 Models","description":"Built for 1977 to race in the national German DRM series under 2 liter class","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1977_Porsche_93520Baby1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1977_Porsche_93520Baby1.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-935-baby/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"Kremer.K2","x":1977,"y":4.135,"name":"Kremer K2","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"Kremer K2","year":1977,"category":"911 Models","description":"Upon seeing the factory 935s and what was possible using the 930 platform, the Kremer bothers from Cologne built up their own version. Their first car contested the World Championship of Makes in 1976 and in the following year, an updated version known as the K2 was further modified.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/1977_Kremer_K21.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/1977_Kremer_K21.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1977-kremer-k2/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"Kremer.K3","x":1977,"y":4.225,"name":"Kremer K3","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"Kremer K3","year":1977,"category":"911 Models","description":"In 1977 Kremer sufficiently improved the 935 to begin series production of their own version. It was the third Kremer built on Porsche’s successful platform and many 935/934s were updated to reflect ideas from the brothers in Cologne.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/Porsche-935-K3.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/Porsche-935-K3.jpeg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1977-kremer-k3/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X911.SC","x":1978,"y":3.775,"name":"911 SC","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 SC","year":1978,"category":"911 Models","description":"Replaced the 911 S and was one of Porsche's first models that was meant for the international market","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/porsche-preview-2.webp","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Porsche-911-SC-Coupe-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-sc-g-series-1978-1983/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"3,000 cc","Power":"182 cv","Torque":"266 Nm","Top speed":"203 km/h","0–100 km/h":"6.5 s","Production":"60,625"}}},{"id":"X924.Rallye.Turbo","x":1978,"y":5,"name":"924 Rallye Turbo","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"924 Rallye Turbo","year":1978,"category":"Race Cars","description":"As a top-secret project, the Porsche Museum workshop and the Porsche Heritage department worked on a special front-engined sports car from 1981. 40 years ago, starting on May 15, this car competed in the Deutsche Rallye-Meisterschaft (German Rally Championship). Behind the wheel was non other than Walter Röhrl, with Christian Geistdörfer next to him. At that time, the two were freshly crowned rally world champions – and unemployed. The reason for this was that Röhrl had signed with Mercedes-Benz. After relatively successful winter tests, however, the fundamentally honest man from Regensburg couldn’t conceal the fact that a victory in the 1981 Monte Carlo Rally would be tantamount to winning the lottery. As a result, the Swabians promptly closed their rally division and Röhrl only just escaped a boring office job. He received his full annual salary and was left without a competitive rally car.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/1_Porsche924CGTSR_25-1024x576.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/1_Porsche924CGTSR_25-1024x576.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-924-rallye-turbo/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / inline-4","Engine":"inline-4","Displacement":"2,000 cc","Power":"213 cv","Torque":null,"Top speed":"225 km/h","0–100 km/h":null,"Production":"5"}}},{"id":"X928","x":1978,"y":3,"name":"928","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"928","year":1978,"category":"Misc Models","description":"In March 1977, the production version of the 928 debuted at the Geneva Motor Show, the first of a new generation of Porsche cars. It had a similar body to the 924, but was powered by a water-cooled V-8 engine. Not only was it a different design compared to the 911 model, but its engine was also cooled differently. Later that year, it became the first sports car to win the European Car of the Year award. In 1978, the 928 was crowned Car of the Year.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1978-1982-Porsche-928-Guide.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Porsche-928-1978-1982-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1978-1982-porsche-928/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / V8","Engine":"V8","Displacement":"4,500 cc","Power":"233 cv","Torque":"343 Nm","Top speed":"230 km/h","0–100 km/h":"7.2 s","Production":null}}},{"id":"X911.SC.Safari","x":1978,"y":3.865,"name":"911 SC Safari","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 SC Safari","year":1978,"category":"911 Models","description":"In 1978 a pair of 911s were entered into the East African Safari Rally.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/HDK-506-38b72fa7.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Porsche-911-SC-Safari-Profile-Small-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-sc-safari/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"3,000 cc","Power":"253 cv","Torque":null,"Top speed":"230 km/h","0–100 km/h":null,"Production":"2"}}},{"id":"X911.Turbo.3.3","x":1978,"y":3.955,"name":"911 Turbo 3.3","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 Turbo 3.3","year":1978,"category":"911 Models","description":"Porsche made its first and most significant upgrades to the 930 for 1978","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1978_Porsche_911Turbo33Coup1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Porsche-911-Turbo-3.3-930-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-turbo-3-3-930/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / flat-6","Engine":"flat-6","Displacement":"3,300 cc","Power":"304 cv","Torque":"412 Nm","Top speed":"257 km/h","0–100 km/h":"5.6 s","Production":"18,770"}}},{"id":"X936.78","x":1978,"y":4.045,"name":"936/78","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"936/78","year":1978,"category":"911 Models","description":"Premiere: 1978 June, Le Mans / Engine: 2.1-litre flat-6, bi-turbo / Gearbox: 5-speed / Achievements: 1978 2nd Place at Le Mans","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/1978-le-mans-start-copyright-porsche-downloaded-from-stuttcars_com.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Porsche-936_78-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-936-78/","specs":{"Layout":"Mid-engine / fla