gine remained unchanged, many improvements were made to the upper end of the engine as well as the drivetrain. On the outside, the car gained alloy wheels, a distinctive NACA duct in its hood, and a rear spoiler. Horsepower increased from 125 bhp to 170 bhp in the turbo. This car’s internal project number at Porsche was “931”, and for that reason many owners refer to their car that as a 931.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Porsche-924-Turbo-Targa-gebraucht-kaufen-Deutschland-33-scaled-1.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Porsche-924-Turbo-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-924-turbo/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / inline-4","Engine":"inline-4","Displacement":"2,000 cc","Power":"112 cv","Torque":"165 Nm","Top speed":"212 km/h","0–100 km/h":"7.1 s","Production":null}}},{"id":"X924.SCCA..924D.","x":1979,"y":5,"name":"924 SCCA (924D)","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"924 SCCA (924D)","year":1979,"category":"Race Cars","description":"These cars were designed by the factory to race in SCCA D Production Championship starting in 1979.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/924-scca-rear-copyright-porsche-downloaded-from-stuttcars_com.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/924-scca-rear-copyright-porsche-downloaded-from-stuttcars_com.jpeg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-924-scca-1979/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"Joest.935J","x":1979,"y":4,"name":"Joest 935J","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"Joest 935J","year":1979,"category":"911 Models","description":"Inspired by the Kremer brothers, Joest built their own version of the 935 for the 1979 season. Like the factory cars it featured intakes in the C-pillars and also had a slightly different front profile. One car was campaigned by Liqui Moly Joest Racing and won the 1980 Daytona 24 Hours outright as driven by Reinhold Jöst, Rolf Stommelen and Volkert Merl. A second car was built up for Electrodyne and raced with Momo livery in the USA. It had a fairly miserable 1980 except the season ender at the Daytona 250 were it won outright. Later in the season it was joined by another 935J. A second car was built up for Electrodyne and raced with Momo livery in the USA. It had a fairly miserable 1980 except the season ender at the Daytona 250 were it won outright. Later in the season it was joined by another 935J. Various other cars were outfitted with the ideas presented by Joest for Spot-Oil-Racing, Vegla Racing Team and DeNarvaez Enterprises for the 1981 season.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/649106.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/649106.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1979-joest-935j/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X924.Carrera.GT","x":1980,"y":2.955,"name":"924 Carrera GT","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"924 Carrera GT","year":1980,"category":"Misc Models","description":"In 1979, Porsche unveiled a concept version of the 924 at the Frankfurt Auto show wearing Carrera badges. One year later, in 1980, Porsche released the 924 Carrera GT, making clear their intention to enter the 924 in competition. By adding an intercooler and increasing compression to 8.5:1, as well as various other little changes, Porsche was able to develop the 924 Turbo into the race car they had wanted, dubbing it the “924 Carrera GT”. 406 examples (including prototypes) of the Carrera GT were built to qualify it for Group 4 racing requirements.[11] Of the 400 roadgoing examples, 75 were made in right hand drive for the UK market. In 1981 Porsche released the limited production 924 Carrera GTS. 59 GTS models were built, all in left hand drive, with 15 of the 59 being raced prepped Clubsport versions.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/Porsche-924-Carrera-GT.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Porsche-924-Carrera-GT-Profile-Large-500x375.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-924-carrera-gt/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / inline-4","Engine":"inline-4","Displacement":"2,000 cc","Power":"213 cv","Torque":"275 Nm","Top speed":"241 km/h","0–100 km/h":"6.7 s","Production":"406"}}},{"id":"X928.S","x":1980,"y":3.045,"name":"928 S","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"928 S","year":1980,"category":"Misc Models","description":"Everything you ever wanted to know about the Porsche 928 S / S2 / S3","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Screen-Shot-2021-05-28-at-3.59.49-PM.png","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/Porsche-928-S-Profile-Large-500x373.png","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1980-1986-porsche-928-s/","specs":{"Layout":"Front-engine / V8","Engine":"V8","Displacement":"4,700 cc","Power":"237 cv","Torque":"357 Nm","Top speed":"230 km/h","0–100 km/h":"6.8 s","Production":null}}},{"id":"X911.SC.Weissach.Edition","x":1980,"y":4,"name":"911 SC Weissach Edition","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"911 SC Weissach Edition","year":1980,"category":"911 Models","description":"The 'Weissach' edition was a standard SC with special paint","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/1980-porsche-911-sc-weissach-edition-8.jpeg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/1980-porsche-911-sc-weissach-edition-8.jpeg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-911-sc-weissach-edition-