ing to the regulations, the body retained the 930 roof structure, but was entirely new from the beltline down. The standard 3.2-liter Porsche engine was used and put out 750 bhp. Leading up to Le Mans the car was raced once at Limerock then sent to France. During the 24 Hour enduro, the car ran out of fuel before one of the drivers even got to race a stint. Later the car competed with Coca-Cola/paradyne livery in America. For the 1983 season it was picked up by Henn’s Swap Shop Racing and raced well into 1984.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/1006977.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/01/1006977.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/1982-gaaco-935l/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X935.Straßenversion","x":1983,"y":4,"name":"935 Straßenversion","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"935 Straßenversion","year":1983,"category":"911 Models","description":"In 1983 Porsche produced a stunning one-off road car for TAG owner Mansour Ojjeh.","image":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1983_Porsche_935Straenversion1.jpg","tooltipImage":"https://www.stuttcars.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/1983_Porsche_935Straenversion1.jpg","url":"https://www.stuttcars.com/porsche-935-strasenversion/","specs":{"Layout":"Rear-engine / Architecture not listed","Engine":"Architecture not listed","Displacement":null,"Power":null,"Torque":null,"Top speed":null,"0–100 km/h":null,"Production":null}}},{"id":"X959.Prototypes","x":1983,"y":-0.045,"name":"959 Prototypes","color":"rgba(64,66,66,0.52)","selected":false,"custom":{"model":"959 Prototypes","year":1983,"category":"Concept Cars","description":"The 1983 Frankfurt Motor Show was chosen for the unveiling of the Porsche Group B prototype. Even in the closing hours of Oc